this chart shows the level of government  borrowing over the last 20 years the  skyscraper was building there or the  financial crisis but they've got nothing  on this this spike shows that the  government borrowed a colossal sixty-two  billion pounds last month that's the  largest number on record it's almost six  times more than in April last year when  11 billion pounds was borrowed and  almost three times the twenty two  billion pounds that was needed in April  2012 which was the peak of borrowing  after the financial crisis now it's a  consequence of two things  government support during the outbreak  and the impact of locked down on tax  receipts the job retention furlough  scheme is currently paying the wages of  8 million people and as so far cost 11  billion pounds and another 2 million  people have claimed self-employment  support that could yet cost another 6  billion on the other side of the  equation the economic slowdown has left  a 25 billion pound hole in government  income from taxes and so on now most of  that is v80 last April the Treasury  collected 12 billion pounds this April  it was actually negative - nought point  nine billion as businesses deferred  paying their VAT and the Treasury  rebated more money than it collected and  of course we are all spending less this  chart shows retail sales volumes they're  down a record 18 percent in April  spending on clothing and shoes was down  52 percent household goods down 45  percent those spending on alcohol and  tobacco was up two percent now the  consequence of all of this is an  historic level of debt what's clear is  that is going to be the biggest deficit  as a share of our economy that we've  seen in peacetime so it will be probably  larger than what we saw the height of  the financial crisis it will probably be  larger than anything we've seen since  the Second World War the irony of these  dreadful figures for the public finance  is they're actually a sign of the  effectiveness of the lock tower measures  and the economic support that's been  offered but now millions of jobs and  livelihoods depend on the government  being just as successful in restarting  the economy
 

